# Deployment

The project must be deployable at any time.

Never introduce deployment-specific hacks.

---

# Environments

Support:

Local

Development

Staging

Production

---

# Promotion path

Every change developed in the local environment must follow Continuous Delivery with human gates for staging and production.

Flow:

Local work

↓

Feature branch

↓

Commit + open PR into `develop` (automatic — do not ask)

↓

Product owner reviews / merges the PR on GitHub

↓

Product owner confirms merge → agent deploys staging (`https://stage.7oz-espresso.com`)

↓

Product owner reviews staging and confirms OK

↓

Product owner opens PR `develop` → `main`

↓

After that PR is merged → agent deploys production (`https://7oz-espresso.com` / `www`) when instructed

Rules:

- Never skip staging for app, API, migration, or Compose changes.
- Do not deploy staging until the product owner confirms the PR is merged into `develop`.
- Do not deploy production until staging is confirmed OK and `main` has the release (product owner owns the `develop` → `main` PR).
- Never deploy production without an explicit instruction after the production release is on `main`.
- Staging and production must use separate secrets, databases, and Compose projects.
- Prefer hotfixes through `develop` → staging → `main`. Deploy a production-only hotfix only when the product owner explicitly instructs it for a critical outage.

---

# Configuration

Never hardcode environment values.

Always use environment variables.

---

# Production

Target Platform:

Ubuntu 24.04 LTS

Nginx

HTTPS

Node.js LTS

Go

PostgreSQL

Redis

---

# Build

Production builds must:

Pass linting.

Pass type checking.

Pass tests.

Complete without warnings where possible.

---

# Secrets

Never commit:

.env

API Keys

JWT Secrets

Database credentials

SMTP credentials

---

# Logging

Separate:

Application logs

Access logs

Error logs

Audit logs

---

# Monitoring

Applications should be ready for:

Health checks

Metrics

Structured logging

Future monitoring integration

---

# Backup

Database backups should be scheduled.

Recovery procedures should be documented.

---

# Goal

Deployment should be repeatable,

predictable,

and reliable.